    "episode_id": 76370,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/6/76370.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Well, Cliff, to begin with President Trump and the Trump administration deserves enormous credit for negotiating these historic agreements in the Abraham Accords. As you said, I was there, one of the highlights over the four years of the Trump administration to be there on hand as the agreements were signed by President Trump. and his counterparts, and what's what's as historic and what's as important as to know as as the, the work that went into negotiating those agreements by the way everyone said it couldn't be done. for years, not just attacking the Trump administration for seeking to negotiate these deals, but mocking the Trump administration. There's no way that Donald Trump can negotiate deals like these. And he ended up doing it. And he never got enough credit for it. But I want to compare how important and significant this agreement was with an article on December 9 of 2020 in the Washington Post. And in that article before President Biden was sworn into office, the President Biden's advisor said that offering a peace plan between Israel and the Palestinians is not a strategic priority. And that should be duly noted today because at this point today, not only is it not a priority for President Biden to further the important work of the Trump administration to put an emphasis on this president Biden still has not yet today, even nominated anyone to serve in the important post as ambassador to Israel, which tells you that the Biden administration is not at all concerned with the nature of the instability in the Middle East or issues at large related to the long-term stability or the partnerships with our most important ally, of all allies, that being Israel. If he's not interested in that relationship or the stability of the Middle East and we have to ask ourselves, what is he interested in?",
