    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/6/76370.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Well, it's such an important question because you have to at the outset, as you ask it, I mean, the you have to understand the paradigm that the Palestinians want to eliminate Israel. They want they want to defeat Israel and they don't they meet it in a in a militaristic way in militaristic terms and they want to wipe Israel off the planet, literally. So by endorsing efforts for Israel to remain victorious, we're not talking about militaristic terms, we're talking about for Israel to prevail, and for it to be continued to be a powerful independent state, and that's why the Trump administration's efforts on this on this path and negotiate the Abraham Accords, and a lot of the other important work that the Trump administration did for four years, it made President Trump the most pro-Israel president that we had ever had. And that put an exclamation mark after that, underline it, no one comes close in being a a pro-Israel advocate as President of the United States, no one comes close to the important work that President Trump and his administration did over four years. So why do the Abraham Accords matter? It mattered. They mattered because of the recognition, because of the Arab countries who joined in this historic agreement with Israel, recognizing Israel's borders and putting pressure on Palestine to recognize Israel's sovereignty and their right to live peacefully. That's why it mattered. And that's why it was historic. That's why it was important. I mean, it had been a generation since another American president had led peace talks like President Trump did. Between the Arab countries who are represented in Israel and again President Trump deserves so much credit for it they never got. Never got it from the mainstream media. He didn't get it publicly it was all it was covered up, but these were his these were historic agreements that mattered greatly. to the sovereignty of Israel. And I hope that history will look back. I know history will look back at these agreements and President Trump's leadership in a way in the decades or generations to come. They will look back very favorably at the work that President Trump did when it came to these peace of courts.",
