  {
    "episode_id": 76370,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/6/76370.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Well, I mean, from the outset, I mean, we're three months into the Biden administration. And the only foreign policy that we've seen from Joe Biden so far is a is a. reversal back to the Obama era policies and the Obama foreign policies, which shouldn't surprise us since Obama filled his cabinet with Obama, this is Obama's third term, when you look at the makeup of the Biden administration. Biden's foreign policy advisors are Obama's foreign policy team. So it shouldn't surprise us that now the posture of the Biden administration is one of appeasement, it's one of leading from behind. is the opposite of the foreign policy that we saw from during the Trump era, which I would I would compare Trump's foreign policy to the to Reagan's piece through strength. The Reagan era foreign policy, there's nothing that comes closer between Reagan and Trump when it comes to their foreign policy, one of strength, one of leadership on the global stage, a realist foreign policy that puts American interest first and ahead of all else and recognizing in that that Israel is our most powerful ally and most important ally globally. So the juxtaposition between the Trump foreign policy and the Biden foreign policy couldn't be any more stark. All of that being said, I mean, in these first three months, it appears that the President Biden has held that upon reentering the disastrous Iran nuclear deal. And the Biden foreign policy so far could be summed up with his path forward on a complete withdraw from Afghanistan and doing it on the anniversary of that tragic day on 9, 11, 20 years later, there couldn't be any worse symbolism, by the way, than completely withdrawing from Afghanistan and allowing the Taliban to declare victory on the day that they precipitated one of the greatest attacks on American soil and American history. So that is the Biden foreign policy and in that It makes it very easy for us as, as Chairman of the Republican Study Committee, as you said, the largest conservative caucus on Capitol Hill, there",
