  {
    "episode_id": 76370,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/6/76370.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Well, I hope the future is different than the past. I mean, for 20 years, we've been doing the same thing over and over again. And as General H. R. McMaster would say, we do it over and over again on an annual basis. So it's like fighting a 20, what will be a 20-year war and fighting it 20 times, year by year, doing the same thing over and over again. I don't have to tell you what the definition of insanity is, but it meets the definition. because we learn the same mistakes over and over again we don't do anything about it. It's my sense though after being on Capitol Hill for four and a half years serving on the Armed Services Committee, serving in Afghanistan in uniform and going back and traveling to Afghanistan as a member of Congress. that it should be all of our goal. Members of Congress, the President, I know it's President Trump's goal. I'm not sure what this will look like on President Biden's watch, but it should be our goal to bring our troops home, but to do so in a responsible way where we leave a stable and strong Afghanistan behind. And by leaving today, we know because America's top military minds have told us that it will be only a matter of days or weeks before the Taliban completely take over Afghanistan once we completely withdraw. And that's why many of us believe and advocated to both President Trump and to President Biden. in an Afghanistan policy of leaving a light footprint with a counter-terrorism focus and get out of the failed nation building attempts that we've made over the last 20 years that have been a colossal failure on all parts and instead focus on doing what we went into Afghanistan to do to begin with, which is to defeat a significant enemy and threat to the United States of America and radical Islamic terrorism, which we see in many forms. From the Taliban to the rise of ISIS-K in Afghanistan is something that does not get enough attention. I know that that you guys have paid a lot of attention to this, but the rise of a much more dangerous ideology of ISIS-K building up in Afghanistan. By us leaving, we're seeding Afghanistan to these terrorist groups who will build Afghanistan back into what it was before September 11, 2001, which was a safe harbor. for terrorists in this region, in this country. We can't afford to do that, or we will be right back where we started. We learned the mistakes of the Obama era when the abrupt withdrawal of Iraq led to the rise of ISIS. President Trump campaigned, by the way, on defeating ISIS. And he did it. He broke up the caliphate in Iraq and Syria. But now we have a growth of ISIS and the branch of ISIS, K, growing in Afghanistan. We can't afford to turn this back over to them again. And we know we know where this will lead. in the aftermath so that and all that to say I mean just I don't want to ramble any longer cliff but that again as someone who served in Afghanistan who served with troops who died and gave the old paid the ultimate sacrifice in Afghanistan. served with those who did. Let me tell you, there's nothing more offensive than the thought of. On September 11th, the Taliban declaring victory 20 years to the day of that tragic event that happened in New York City when I was a college student watching on television, what inspired me to serve my country in a big way afterward. There couldn't be any worse imagery, any worse symbolism than what will occur if Joe Biden allows that to happen.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
