  {
    "episode_id": 76370,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/6/76370.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "If the president and his administration are going to circumvent Congress, we have a constitutional responsibility to fill the void, to deal with what we're supposed to do to begin with and assert our role ourselves, especially if the president is trying to take it away from us. But as far as the long-term goal, if President Biden is going to reenter this disastrous deal and appease and reward Iran for their bad behavior, then Congress needs to step up to the plate and do something about it. But let's take a step back and remember that President Trump issued 1,500 sanction designations on Iran and five executive orders expanding those sanctions. And as a result of those sanctions, we know that Iran's foreign currency reserves dropped 95%. That's why I said a little bit ago that the maximum pressure campaign worked. The Iranian military budget dropped by 25% because we dried up their resources and their proxy groups and their militias had to cut their fighters' salaries in half as a result. So with the maximum pressure campaign that we introduced yesterday, 90 co-sponsors and growing a product of our work with Republican Study Committee, We codify the maximum pressure campaign that Mike, really in the spirit of Mike Pompeo's work as Secretary of State, that he wrote, we codify that in the law to determine the foreign policy moving forward. We should never reward Iran for their bad behavior. By doing so, we show weakness and we invite more of their malign activities to occur in the future. And when we're talking about foreign policy at large, it's the opposite of what America's foreign policy should look like, especially in the Middle East.",
